[{"result":{"data":{"json":{"id":"273","stub":{"id":"273","section":"Pieta Creek to US 101 Bridge","altname":"Frog Woman Rock Run","river":"Russian River","difficulty":"IIplusstandoutIII","states":["USA-CAL"]},"primaryGaugeStatus":{"status":"below-recommended","latestReading":{"value":"158","dateTime":"2026-05-12T02:00:00.000Z","qualifier":"NORMAL"},"metric":"cfs","adjustedDifficulty":null},"pointOfInterests":[{"type":"put-in","description":null,"id":"111626","location":{"latitude":"38.9209","longitude":"-123.059"},"name":"Put In","distance":"0","photoID":null,"difficulty":"N/A","approximate":false},{"type":"waterfall","description":"This is the only Class III. The water drops significantly as it makes a large bend around a 500 foot high rock. There are several drops. The biggest involves fast manuvering around a large rock at low water. Above 1500 or so CFS, the rock forms a pretty much unavoidable hole.","id":"337","location":{"latitude":"38.914283752441406","longitude":"-123.05585479736328"},"name":"Frog Woman Rock","distance":"1","difficulty":"III","approximate":false,"photoID":"1188"},{"type":"rapid","description":"This is a relatively high gradient rock garden directly below Frog Woman Rock. The five-foot drop at the end is often called \"Tilden Falls.\"","id":"333","location":{"latitude":"38.906833","longitude":"-123.058305"},"name":"Grave Yard","distance":"1.3","difficulty":"II+","approximate":false,"photoID":null},{"type":"playspot","description":"There is a great \"Splat Rock\" just below the low-water bridge at Comminsky Station. Great fun.","id":"343","location":{"latitude":"0","longitude":"0"},"name":"Comminsky Station Splat Rock","distance":"2.5","difficulty":null,"approximate":false,"photoID":null},{"type":"rapid","description":"The river splits around a large rock. The right-most channel takes you down a narrow \"Hallway\" into \"The Room.\" Splatrock and hole at the bottom.\n\nThe left channel is also fun if it isn't congested with strainers. Be careful.","id":"344","location":{"latitude":"0","longitude":"0"},"name":"The Room","distance":"2.7","difficulty":"II+","approximate":false,"photoID":null},{"type":"rapid","description":"Two consecutive drops. Big rocks in mid stream. 2nd drop has a good play hole at moderate flows.","id":"342","location":{"latitude":"0","longitude":"0"},"name":"Double-Drop","distance":"3","difficulty":"II+","approximate":false,"photoID":"1189"},{"type":"rapid","description":"Actually two distinct drops. Take the left most branch after the big pool at \"Alcapulco\" (Named for the big Pyramid Shaped diving rock on river right). The first is called \"The Ledge\" and is a steep chute with a 2 - 3 ft drop. This is a hole at higher water.\n\nAfter a short easy section, the left-side-stream merges with the main river for a congested rapid. Holes at moderate water, washed-out at high water.","id":"334","location":{"latitude":"0","longitude":"0"},"name":"C-Turn","distance":"4","difficulty":"II+","approximate":false,"photoID":"1191"},{"type":"takeout","description":null,"id":"116899","location":{"latitude":"38.8585","longitude":"-123.04"},"name":"Take Out","distance":"8.4","photoID":null,"difficulty":"N/A","approximate":false}],"alerts":[],"photos":[{"id":"884567","ext":"jpeg","post_id":"887273","description":"","caption":"","poi_name":null,"subject":null,"author":"","poi_id":null,"photo_date":null,"updated_at":null,"url":{"medium":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/medium/884567","large":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/big/884567"}},{"id":"wK5VXv1ZWoRy3DdBzuzhg","ext":"jpg","post_id":"L4vndphMuQX0Z3rhrwbUK","description":"Dup Crosson, Veronica Yovovich, Trent Pearce","caption":"","poi_name":null,"subject":"","author":"Dup Crosson","poi_id":null,"photo_date":"2019-01-27T01:11:39.000Z","updated_at":"2023-11-06T01:11:39.000Z","url":{"medium":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/medium/wK5VXv1ZWoRy3DdBzuzhg","large":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/big/wK5VXv1ZWoRy3DdBzuzhg"}},{"id":"8","ext":"jpeg","post_id":"2896","description":"These \"Knockers\" in the stream bed are fun to look at.  They have very convoluted layers and have been worn very smooth.","caption":"Quiet stretch above Squaw Rock","poi_name":"","subject":"","author":"Randy Hodges","poi_id":null,"photo_date":null,"updated_at":null,"url":{"medium":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/medium/8","large":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/big/8"}},{"id":"wlz5aomq5rbYPooOGNsuT","ext":"jpg","post_id":"L4vndphMuQX0Z3rhrwbUK","description":"","caption":"","poi_name":null,"subject":"","author":"Dup Crosson","poi_id":null,"photo_date":"2019-01-27T01:11:39.000Z","updated_at":"2023-11-06T01:11:39.000Z","url":{"medium":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/medium/wlz5aomq5rbYPooOGNsuT","large":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/big/wlz5aomq5rbYPooOGNsuT"}},{"id":"884570","ext":"jpeg","post_id":"887276","description":"","caption":"Tilden Falls","poi_name":null,"subject":null,"author":"","poi_id":null,"photo_date":null,"updated_at":null,"url":{"medium":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/medium/884570","large":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/big/884570"}},{"id":"884569","ext":"jpeg","post_id":"887275","description":"","caption":"","poi_name":null,"subject":null,"author":"","poi_id":null,"photo_date":null,"updated_at":null,"url":{"medium":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/medium/884569","large":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/big/884569"}},{"id":"887294","ext":"tiff","post_id":"889990","description":"750cfs","caption":"","poi_name":null,"subject":null,"author":"","poi_id":null,"photo_date":null,"updated_at":null,"url":{"medium":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/medium/887294","large":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/big/887294"}},{"id":"1190","ext":"jpeg","post_id":"2896","description":"","caption":"\"The Ledge\" at C-Turn","poi_name":"C-Turn","subject":"William Hodges","author":"Chuck Bartley","poi_id":"334","photo_date":null,"updated_at":null,"url":{"medium":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/medium/1190","large":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/big/1190"}},{"id":"1191","ext":"jpeg","post_id":"2897","description":"This is the first drop of C-Turn.  It would be easy to miss.  The natural way to run this rapid is by going right.  This left-hand channel is hidden at most levels.  Big fun.  Good play spot.","caption":"\"The Ledge\" at C-Turn","poi_name":"C-Turn","subject":"William Hodges","author":"Chuck Bartley","poi_id":"334","photo_date":null,"updated_at":null,"url":{"medium":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/medium/1191","large":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/big/1191"}},{"id":"1188","ext":"jpeg","post_id":"2898","description":"This is a pretty intimidating drop at high water.  A big drop and hole are just beyond the horizon line.","caption":"Squaw Rock on the Russian River","poi_name":"Squaw Rock","subject":"","author":"Chuck Bartley","poi_id":"344","photo_date":null,"updated_at":null,"url":{"medium":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/medium/1188","large":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/big/1188"}},{"id":"1189","ext":"jpeg","post_id":"2898","description":"Double-Drop is pretty much washed out at this level.","caption":"Double-Drop at high water","poi_name":"Double Drop","subject":"Randy Hodges in the raft.  Miles Everette in the IK","author":"Chuck Bartley","poi_id":"342","photo_date":null,"updated_at":null,"url":{"medium":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/medium/1189","large":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/big/1189"}}],"detail":{"id":"273","permitUrl":"","permitInfo":"","description":"This is commonly called the Frog Woman Rock section where the river flows by the base of a prominent volcanic bluff by this name. The largest rapid is also at the base of this bluff. Frog Woman Rock is the anglicized version of the local Pomo Indian name for this rock formation; it has been previously referred to by a derogatory name.\n\nThere are many alternate put-ins and take-outs here. The section from Pieta Creek to Comminsky Station is pretty flat except for Frog Woman Rock itself. You might consider entering at Comminsky instead. We often do the 1.5 Mile stretch from Comminsky to just below C-Turn. We feel that this short stretch has the best playing potential. There is a large turn-out from the freeway there and you will often see parked cars with racks on them.\n\nYou might also consider taking out at NACO or Preston. Both are considerably easier take-outs than the bridge. The bridge take out involves either private property (occasionally enforced) or a steep artificial talus slope. [Take out (googlemap)](http://maps.google.com/maps?q=38.8330994,+-123.0108032+(Russian+River+take+out)&hl=en&ie=UTF8&z=17&ll=,38.8330994-123.0108032&spn=0.00376,0.010632&t=h&om=1)\n\nThe Frog Woman Rock Rapid is the only true Class III here. It is considerably harder when the flow is below 300 or above 1500. Above 2000, it is probably a Class IV. You may scout this rapid from the road before you run it.\n\nThe 'C-Turn' rapid can be Class III-/III at flows greater than 700 or so CFS. Be sure to take the left-hand channel from the large pool above.\n\nWarning - This section has had a number of trees (strainers) across the river in some years.\n\n[Frog Woman Rock](http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Frog_Woman_Rock) is the angclicized version of original Pomo Indian name for this volcanic rock. In the Pomo language it is called Bi-tsin’ ma-ca Ka-be’ (Frog Woman Cliff). Frog Woman is an important figure in Pomo traditional beliefs. She has a beautiful face but the body of a frog, and lives in a den in the rock. She can leap a hundred feet to snatch and devour men. Pomo people avoid the rock for fear of being eaten by her.","averageGradient":"15","maxGradient":"50","length":"5.4","correlations":[{"reachID":"273","isPrimary":true,"correlationDetails":{"metric":"cfs","belowRecommendedRangeComment":null,"lowRunnableRangeComment":null,"mediumRunnableRangeComment":null,"highRunnableRangeComment":null,"aboveRecommendedRangeComment":null,"lowRunnableAdjustedDifficulty":null,"highRunnableAdjustedDifficulty":null,"beginLowRunnable":"200","beginMediumRunnable":null,"beginHighRunnable":null,"endHighRunnable":"30000"},"gaugeInfo":{"name":"RUSSIAN R NR CLOVERDALE CA","gaugeSource":"USGS","gaugeSourceIdentifier":"11463000","gaugeFetchStatus":"ACTIVE","latestFlowReading":{"value":"158","dateTime":"2026-05-12T02:00:00.000Z","qualifier":"NORMAL"},"latestStageReading":{"value":"2.76","dateTime":"2026-05-12T02:00:00.000Z","qualifier":"NORMAL"},"location":{"latitude":"38.87930758","longitude":"-123.0535767"},"externalSourceLinks":{"sourceLink":{"url":"https://water.noaa.gov/gauges/11463000","text":"NOAA"},"secondaryLink":{"url":"https://waterdata.usgs.gov/monitoring-location/11463000/#parameterCode=00060&period=P7D","text":"US Geological Survey"}}},"migrationErrorExplanation":null,"comment":"Gauge (503 sq.mi. drainage) is about midway on this reach, thus should accurately show actual flow in this reach.","status":{"status":"below-recommended","latestReading":{"value":"158","dateTime":"2026-05-12T02:00:00.000Z","qualifier":"NORMAL"},"metric":"cfs","adjustedDifficulty":null}}],"pointOfInterestIDs":["111626","337","333","343","344","342","334","116899"],"geometry":{"type":"LineString","coordinates":[[-123.059,38.9209],[-123.059049,38.920856],[-123.059426,38.920165],[-123.059733,38.919551],[-123.05979,38.919222],[-123.059854,38.918059],[-123.059877,38.917771],[-123.060037,38.91758],[-123.060097,38.91736],[-123.060043,38.916973],[-123.059941,38.916652],[-123.059799,38.916456],[-123.059584,38.916278],[-123.059421,38.916153],[-123.059076,38.916084],[-123.058695,38.915885],[-123.058415,38.91579],[-123.057906,38.915612],[-123.05755,38.9153